    desc: "In Kalotaszeg, a cluster of Transylvanian Hungarian villages west of Cluj, linen pillowcases, aprons, altar cloths and dress panels are worked with richly curved flowers — tulips, pinks, carnations, roses and small birds — outlined in chain stitch and filled in satin stitch. The palette is famously disciplined: strong red against strong blue, later softened with greens and golds, always on bleached homespun. Variants range from free-flowing piecework to the counted írások style, and 19th-century dowry inventories trace its spread as wedding wealth. The craft survived collectivisation and exile, kept alive by village embroiderers, Hungarian folk-art revivalists and museum collections in Cluj and Budapest. Today it dresses Pentecost processions, decorates Calvinist and Catholic interiors alike, and supplies a heritage market of reconstructed folk costume.",
