    desc: "The Transylvanian Saxons, colonists settled from the twelfth century, furnished their houses with chest-built Kasten dowry chests, cupboards, cradles and benches painted by village workshops that peaked in the 1800s. Motifs blend late Baroque flowers — tulips, pinks, acanthus — with the community's emblem, the Engelsherz or angels' heart, plus inscription panels, marbleising and grained borders in blue-greens, red-browns and ochres. Pieces marked betrothals and household rank, travelled with brides, and were periodically repainted by itinerant masters. The twentieth-century exodus of nearly all Saxons emptied the villages, so painted furniture now survives largely in museums and restored house ensembles, above all Sibiu's ASTRA complex and the furnished Saxon rooms of villages like Viscri, where guesthouses keep chests in daily use.",
