    p: "Venetian era onward; UNESCO inscription 2009", era: "late medieval to living craft",
    sum: "Lefkaritika is Lefkara's white-on-white lacework, a Venetian-era blend of drawn-thread embroidery and needlelace that Leonardo's 1481 visit made legendary.",
    desc: "In Lefkara's cobbled lanes women work lefkaritika: tablecloths, napkins and altar linen patterned by cutting and drawing out threads, then darning and overcasting the remainder, and finished with sprays of floral needlelace — the ploumisto style — all strictly white or ivory. Venetian trade carried the work to aristocratic Europe, and village tradition credits Leonardo da Vinci with buying an altar cloth for Milan Cathedral during a 1481 visit. Mass tourism and migration thinned the ranks of embroiderers, prompting UNESCO's 2009 inscription of Lefkara laces (Lefkaritika) on the Representative List. A village women's cooperative and the Cyprus Handicraft Service now train new generations, certify authentic pieces with sewn labels, and keep the summer-doorway stool-and-hoop image of the village alive for visitors.",
