    sum: "Greek island folk dress layers silk chemises, short embroidered jackets and heavy silver into regionally coded ensembles, crowned by the Amalia dress myth of 1836.",
    desc: "Island costume encoded marriage status, island and wealth in layers: a white silk chemise, the short wool sigouni jacket trimmed with gold braid, the dark kavadi coat, and silver chest chains and brooches. Karpathos' mountain village Olympos keeps the most exuberant dress, with multicoloured aprons and gold-stitched panels worn at weddings and panigyria. Men wore vraka breeches — Cretan dark wool versions with a tucked knife, sailor variants on Hydra. Queen Amalia's 1836 court dress, a blue-silk gown with loose jacket and tasselled cap, fused Bavarian and island elements into a promoted national dress spread by illustrated press. The Lykeion ton Ellinidon, founded in 1911, records and stages authentic costumes, which now anchor festivals, weddings and the great costume collections of Athens museums.",
