    o: "Faroe Islands, North Atlantic", g: "all eighteen Faroese islands, notably Tórshavn and the outports",
    p: "medieval wool export to living national dress", era: "Norse settlement to living tradition",
    sum: "On the Faroes, where sheep long outnumber people, wool runs through everything from everyday knitwear to the silver-buckled national dress worn at Ólavsøka.",
    desc: "Sheep gave the Faroes both name (Færeyjar, sheep islands) and subsistence: home-grown wool was carded, spun and knitted in every household into jumpers, caps and the famous patterned shawls whose winged shoulders grip the wearer. Exported sweaters with bird, flower and wave motifs carried the islands' knitting worldwide in the mid-twentieth century, later amplified by design houses such as Guðrun & Guðrun. Wool also dresses the nation: the national costume pairs hand-knit stockings and waistcoats with silver buttons and, for women, silver-trimmed headdresses and shawls, worn en masse at Ólavsøka on 29 July. Spinning wheels, dye pots and wool stores remain common sights, and knitwear cooperatives keep the chain local from clip to jumper in a place where weather makes wool a necessity, not a nostalgia.",
