    p: "pre-Christian roots; folk revival documented since the 19th century", era: "pagan-continuum to national tradition",
    sum: "Latvia's Jāņi midsummer pairs bonfires, wreaths and caraway cheese with an ancient sign vocabulary — Māra's cross, Jumis, Auseklis — woven into belts, mittens and song.",
    desc: "At Jāņi (23-24 June) Latvians wear oak-leaf and flower wreaths, brew beer, bake Jāņu siers caraway cheese and sing līgo refrains through the shortest night around bonfires, floating wreaths downstream. The festival carries a geometric sign language: Māra's cross guarding hearth and house, Jumis' doubled spike for fertility, Auseklis the morning star, sun discs and stars, worked into woven josta belts, mittens and sashes. Krišjānis Barons' Dainu skapis — a cabinet of over 217,000 archived folk-song texts, itself on UNESCO's Memory of the World register — codified the repertoire these signs travel with. Soviet bans only deepened the songs' political charge, and the restored Song and Dance Festival now stages the whole sign-and-song system at national scale in Riga.",
