    sum: "The Setos, an Orthodox Finno-Ugric people of borderland Setomaa, keep leelo polyphony, tsässon chapels and towering silver jewellery in a culture UNESCO lists as urgently safeguarded.",
    desc: "Straddling the Estonia-Russia frontier, the Setos joined a Finnic language to Orthodox faith from medieval times, building wooden tsässon village chapels and keeping a calendar of Orthodox-folk feasts. Their leelo — led by an improvising sõnoline soloist over a drone — is a polyphony UNESCO inscribed on the Urgent Safeguarding List in 2009. Costume is unmistakable: linen shirts under wool, crowned by massive silver sõlg brooches with dangling chains that announced family wealth, worn at their most abundant by brides. Since the 1990s the community has re-crowned a symbolic Seto Kingdom at Obinitsa each August, complete with king and suur sõlg. Border changes after 1991 split families and villages, making leelo choirs, language camps and museum work matters of cultural survival.",
