    o: "Cornwall and west Devon, United Kingdom", g: "Cornish mining districts: Redruth, Camborne, St Agnes, Botallack, Levant, Tavistock fringe",
    p: "1712-1930s, mostly 19th century", era: "Industrial Revolution",
    sum: "Cornish engine houses are the granite-and-brick shells built to hold beam engines that kept Cornish tin and copper mines dry, now ruins strung along a UNESCO-listed landscape.",
    desc: "Every deep Cornish mine needed a beam engine to lift water, and each engine got a tapered granite house with brick arches, a bob-wall aperture over the shaft and a tall slim stack, often stepped into cliff or moor. Built chiefly in the 1800s to house ever-larger high-pressure Cornish engines — the most efficient steam engines of their day — the houses hardened into a standard pattern repeated across the mining districts. When copper collapsed and tin slumped, the pumps stilled and the houses were left as landmarks; the Cornwall and West Devon Mining Landscape won World Heritage status in 2006 across ten areas. Emigrant Cousin Jacks rebuilt the type in Mexico, Australia, South Africa and Michigan, and the cliff-top ruins at Botallack and Wheal Coates keep the style famous on screen and page.",
