    sum: "The Celtic Revival reworked Insular knotwork and stone-cross carving into modern design, most elegantly in Archibald Knox's Cymric silver and Tudric pewter for Liberty & Co.",
    desc: "Fired by antiquarian study of illuminated gospels, high crosses and brooches, designers across Britain and Ireland translated interlace, spirals and engraved zoomorphs into contemporary wares. The decisive figure was Manx-born Archibald Knox (1864-1933), who from 1899 designed Liberty & Co.'s Cymric silver and Tudric pewter: clean rectilinear forms skinned with fine knotwork and often left partly unornamented, bridging Arts and Crafts restraint and Art Nouveau line. In Dublin the Dun Emer and Cuala presses and allied metalwork shops gave the revival an Irish-national cast, while Knox's manuscript-style lettering albums seeded Manx design identity. The movement fed early twentieth-century jewellery, book covers and stained glass, and its museum-friendly canon endures in reproductions, wedding bands and heritage branding across the Celtic countries.",
    col: [{ h: "#8c8f94", n: "pewter grey" }, { h: "#c9ccd1", n: "worked silver" }, { h: "#3f6b52", n: "Celtic enamel green" }, { h: "#c39a4e", n: "manuscript ochre" }, { h: "#efe8d4", n: "vellum cream" }],
