    cat: "Religious & Sacred Art", c: "Insular ecclesiastical goldsmithing", e: "historical",
    o: "early medieval Ireland", g: "Ireland; National Museum of Ireland, Dublin",
    p: "7th-9th century golden age, decline after 12th-century church reform", era: "early medieval",
    sum: "Early Irish monasteries produced Europe's most sophisticated metalwork — the Ardagh Chalice, Tara Brooch and Derrynaflan hoard — fusing gold filigree, enamel and amber into Insular splendour.",
    desc: "Between the seventh and ninth centuries Irish monastic workshops raised goldsmithing to unmatched heights. The Ardagh Chalice (early eighth century), found in 1868 at Reerasta, joins beaten silver with gilt-bronze bands, filigree interlace panels, enamels, amber and glass studs — hundreds of individual fittings hiding under a serene curve. The Tara Brooch (c. 700), ploughed up at Bettystown in 1850, packs a pseudo-penannular form with gold filigree, amber and millefiori. The 1980 Derrynaflan find — chalice, paten and sieve — confirmed a whole school. Techniques included drawn-wire filigree, chip-carving, cloisonné and gilding, serving church ritual and elite display alike. After the twelfth-century church reform the school faded, leaving objects now centred in the National Museum of Ireland and endlessly reinterpreted by the Celtic Revival.",
    col: [{ h: "#b9bec4", n: "beaten silver" }, { h: "#c8a13f", n: "gilt gold" }, { h: "#8f2f2a", n: "enamel red" }, { h: "#cf8a2e", n: "amber" }, { h: "#3d5a4a", n: "patina green" }],
