    o: "Irish and Northumbrian monasteries", g: "Iona, Kells, Lindisfarne, Durrow; Trinity College Dublin and British Library collections",
    p: "7th-9th centuries", era: "early medieval",
    sum: "Insular manuscript illumination turned gospel books into labyrinths of interlace, carpet pages and exploding initials, culminating in the Book of Kells around 800.",
    desc: "Monasteries of Ireland and Northumbria developed a gospel-book art unlike any contemporary: full pages of pure pattern — carpet pages of spirals, knots and stepped crosses — and initials that detonate into men, beasts and endless cords, as on Kells' Chi-Rho page. The Lindisfarne Gospels (c. 698), written and painted by Eadfrith, the Book of Durrow and the Book of Kells (c. 800) set the canon, with blues, yellows and reds laid flat on calfskin vellum. Scribes mixed La Tène-derived ornament with Mediterranean book design, creating a style carried by missionaries across Europe that influenced continental illumination. After Viking raids dispersed the communities, the great books survived as relics — Kells now in Trinity College Dublin, Lindisfarne in the British Library — among the most visited artworks of the British Isles.",
