    o: "Carpathian mountains of western Ukraine and adjacent Romania", g: "Ivano-Frankivsk and Zakarpattia oblasts, Bukovina, and Suceava county, Romania",
    p: "documented from the 18th century; living tradition", era: "early modern to living tradition",
    sum: "Hutsul highlanders of the Carpathians built a culture of inlaid wood, painted ceramics, trembita horns and keptar vests celebrated across Ukraine.",
    desc: "Hutsuls — sheepherding highlanders of the eastern Carpathians — turned mountain materials into signature crafts: chests, powder horns and small topirky axes carved and inlaid with contrasting woods, beads and metal; Kosiv's painted ceramic tiles and plates with horses, roosters and garden trees, inscribed by UNESCO in 2019; and Hutsul pysanky Easter eggs in dense red-black writing. Shepherd life gave the trembita, an alpine horn up to three metres long that once carried signals between valleys, plus the keptar vest, felt hats and embroidered shirts worn with wide leather belts. Kolomyia's National Museum of Hutsulshchyna and Pokuttya Folk Art holds the canon, Hutsul fiddle-and-drymba music fills festivals, and the culture now drives Carpathian tourism, film and craft revival on both sides of the border.",
    col: [{ h: "#5d4327", n: "walnut brown" }, { h: "#9a3b26", n: "Kosiv red-brown" }, { h: "#a92c22", n: "egg red" }, { h: "#b08d3e", n: "brass inlay" }, { h: "#3e5a3c", n: "spruce green" }],
