    desc: "In Sorbian villages between Bautzen and Cottbus, decorating eggs is a community art passed down in families and clubs. Several techniques coexist: wax batik drawn with pin-heads and goose quills, engraving scratched through dyed colour, and etching. Motifs — suns, roosters, flowers, waves and geometric crosses — belong to a shared West Slavic sign vocabulary, worked in reds, blacks and greens over natural brown or blown white shells, the finest pieces rivalling lace. Eggs are exchanged at Easter, entered at village competitions and Easter markets, and collected by institutions such as the Sorbian Museum in Bautzen. Around the craft lives the wider Sorbian Easter, including the Osterreiter, Easter-morning riders who process between villages carrying the news of the resurrection.",
