    desc: "Marionette theatre took root in Bohemia in the eighteenth century with itinerant dynasties like the Kopecký family, playing folk-cast operas and comedies in village barns and inns — and, under a German-language theatre monopoly, giving Czech its own stage voice. Carved linden marionettes with jointed, expressive faces became the craft's signature. The twentieth century added Josef Skupa's comic schoolmaster Spejbl (1920) and rascal Hurvínek (1926), radio and film stars whose Prague theatre still tours. The National Marionette Theatre in Prague has staged Mozart's Don Giovanni with marionettes since 1991, Plzeň keeps a dedicated puppet museum, and UNESCO inscribed Puppet Theatre in Slovakia and Czechia on the Representative List in 2016. Carving workshops now supply both heritage stages and a global collector market.",
