    sum: "The fujara, an up-to-two-metre overtone bass flute from Podpoľanie, turns Slovak shepherd solitude into UNESCO-listed music.",
    desc: "The fujara is a contrabass duct flute of 160-200 cm, played upright with a small parallel mouth tube feeding the main elder-wood bore, its three finger-holes coaxing deep, breathy overtones. Born among Podpoľanie shepherds around Detva and Očová who guarded dispersed flocks on the poľana hills, it was the lone herdsman's instrument for signals and long improvised meditations — typically a slow solemn prelude, a rippling middle and a calm close. Players decorate the wood with carved and painted folk ornament. UNESCO proclaimed fujara music a Masterpiece in 2005 and inscribed Fujara Flute and its Music on the Representative List in 2008; the fujara now stars at the Východná and Detva folk festivals and in world-music ensembles, its relative the koncovka keeping the overtone tradition alive in miniature.",
